      "text": "Time 1-1.5h ( 90 mins ) \n\nCompany: phonepe, flipkart, swiggy, razorpay etc. \n\n\nObjective: \n-> aim is to cover all the requirements. \n-> aim is to code to be runnable as per those requirements \n    and as per some test cases of interviewer as well. \n-> aim code should be module, extendable following\n    SOLID, design patterns.\n-> some time interview ask to write testcases as well ( rare ) \n-> after writing this usually we do have review discussion for this.\n-> some time we also have some bonuses requirements. \n->  evaluate your coding skills and how efficient you are in writing production-grade code\n-> You should be very comfortable in doing mundane things like creating interfaces, creating singletons (if required), \n    implementing some sort of dependency injection, creating custom comparators, avoiding code duplication, \n    refactoring, etc. This shows that you actively write code on a daily basis.\n-> You should know how to take advantage of the APIs exposed by the language like getting the current timestamp, \n    checking if a list/string is empty or null, and I/O operations to name a few. The thing is interviewer might not mind \n    if you query the internet on how to read a file, but if you have to do that first, you are wasting your time\n    and secondly, if are still not able to do that, you might as well call it a day. \n    In general, try to avoid Googling \"How to do something in some programming language\" \n    in front of your interviewer. If you absolutely have to do this, try checking the official documentation site \n    of the language API. It gives the impression that you are not copying and pasting someone else's code blindly. \n-> In order to come up with and implement the NFRs in your code, you must have a good idea of the concurrency \n    and threading model in the language you are using\n-> You should be able to implement some of the concurrency principles in your code. You should be aware \n    of things like how to start threads, how to communicate between different threads, and how to \n    synchronize between multiple threads. You should have a good understanding of mutexes, semaphores, \n    locks, deadlocks, and race conditions. You should also know how to handle them in code. If you do, do \n    it correctly and are able to answer questions that the interviewer might ask to follow up, it does not just\n    help you ace this round, it is going to increase your rating. This increased rating will help even out any bad rounds.\n-> \n\n\nRejection Cases: \n-> Struggling a lot while implementing your code, making a lot of syntax errors even for minor things \n   like switch-case statements, or while defining interfaces, etc.\n-> Struggling to create common data structures like Hashmaps or PriorityQueues. You are getting \n   a lot of errors while implementing these or have to check the documentation.\n-> You have to check documentation again and again for common operations like how to find the current \n   timestamp, how to check if a key is present in the map, how to create a parent-child relationship between classes, etc.\n-> If you have explained one approach but while implementing you coded something entirely different. \n   This is a very bad signal, it gives multiple red signs like while creating the solution you were not sure about the \n   actual problem and now you have to make changes in your solution or you don't know how to implement \n   what you just explained. Take enough time at the start of the interview to understand the problem well, \n   ask clarifying questions if required, come up with a solution, check edge cases, and then when you are confident,\n   start coding. Avoid things in your solution that you are not comfortable with.\n-> no following oops, coding style, not extendable, \n-> Finally, manage your time well. Don't waste time doing things that are not necessary. \n    Consult your interviewer regularly while coding\n-> Write testable code. You should be able to write unit tests if asked.\n \nAssumption:\n    -> in memory data not database client or connection. \n    -> also usually don't ask for api / route. \n\n\n\n\n1. Requirements \n    -> most of the time requirement are given , usually ita a big statement shared in google doc. \n    -> sample: check google doc, or pdf in lld section.\n    -> some give it might not give like design uber lld etc. \n    -> in those cases extract all the information , keeping in mind\n        that we need to implement it not like high level desing.\n    -> no need to ask the nfr etc. \n\n\n2. Understand the requirements: -> 10 -15mins\n\n\n\n3. Coding:  -> 40-50 minutes.\n\nUseful things: \n    -> in this cases usually we don't have complex implementation like dsa.\n    -> also in some cases its also fine if not very optimized , its should be running.\n       but yes exception are there, problem can be tough as well as\n       interviewer can ask for optimisation or optimize solution. \n\n    -> require oops language. \n    -> in this problem we might also need to deal with.\n        -> parsing, time, marshing, logging, string manipulation, input\n            output, random, math etc.\n    -> some time in this questions people also ask for api implementation\n        basically on the top of controller implement the apis. \n        -> sharechat. \n\n    -> you can even golang since you are writing code in golang in production\n        -> just make sure , fix one libraray for stl, since we can use the ide here\n            so its ok to using third party libraray for stl, they are not interested\n            to know whether you are able to writing code for sorting algo or red black tree. \n  ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Time 1-1.5h ( 90 mins ) \n\nCompany: phonepe, flipkart, swiggy, razorpay etc. \n\n\nObjective: \n-> aim is to cover all the requirements. \n-> aim is to code to be runnable as per those requirements \n    and as per some test cases of interviewer as well. \n-> aim code should be module, extendable following\n    SOLID, design patterns.\n-> some time interview ask to write testcases as well ( rare ) \n-> after writing this usually we do have review discussion for this.\n-> some time we also have some bonuses requirements. \n->  evaluate your coding skills and how efficient you are in writing production-grade code\n-> You should be very comfortable in doing mundane things like creating interfaces, creating singletons (if required), \n    implementing some sort of dependency injection, creating custom comparators, avoiding code duplication, \n    refactoring, etc. This shows that you actively write code on a daily basis.\n-> You should know how to take advantage of the APIs exposed by the language like getting the current timestamp, \n    checking if a list/string is empty or null, and I/O operations to name a few. The thing is interviewer might not mind \n    if you query the internet on how to read a file, but if you have to do that first, you are wasting your time\n    and secondly, if are still not able to do that, you might as well call it a day. \n    In general, try to avoid Googling \"How to do something in some programming language\" \n    in front of your interviewer. If you absolutely have to do this, try checking the official documentation site \n    of the language API. It gives the impression that you are not copying and pasting someone else's code blindly. \n-> In order to come up with and implement the NFRs in your code, you must have a good idea of the concurrency \n    and threading model in the language you are using\n-> You should be able to implement some of the concurrency principles in your code. You should be aware \n    of things like how to start threads, how to communicate between different threads, and how to \n    synchronize between multiple threads. You should have a good understanding of mutexes, semaphores, \n    locks, deadlocks, and race conditions. You should also know how to handle them in code. If you do, do \n    it correctly and are able to answer questions that the interviewer might ask to follow up, it does not just\n    help you ace this round, it is going to increase your rating. This increased rating will help even out any bad rounds.\n-> \n\n\nRejection Cases: \n-> Struggling a lot while implementing your code, making a lot of syntax errors even for minor things \n   like switch-case statements, or while defining interfaces, etc.\n-> Struggling to create common data structures like Hashmaps or PriorityQueues. You are getting \n   a lot of errors while implementing these or have to check the documentation.\n-> You have to check documentation again and again for common operations like how to find the current \n   timestamp, how to check if a key is present in the map, how to create a parent-child relationship between classes, etc.\n-> If you have explained one approach but while implementing you coded something entirely different. \n   This is a very bad signal, it gives multiple red signs like while creating the solution you were not sure about the \n   actual problem and now you have to make changes in your solution or you don't know how to implement \n   what you just explained. Take enough time at the start of the interview to understand the problem well, \n   ask clarifying questions if required, come up with a solution, check edge cases, and then when you are confident,\n   start coding. Avoid things in your solution that you are not comfortable with.\n-> no following oops, coding style, not extendable, \n-> Finally, manage your time well. Don't waste time doing things that are not necessary. \n    Consult your interviewer regularly while coding\n-> Write testable code. You should be able to write unit tests if asked.\n \nAssumption:\n    -> in memory data not database client or connection. \n    -> also usually don't ask for api / route. \n\n\n\n\n1. Requirements \n    -> most of the time requirement are given , usually ita a big statement shared in google doc. \n    -> sample: check google doc, or pdf in lld section.\n    -> some give it might not give like design uber lld etc. \n    -> in those cases extract all the information , keeping in mind\n        that we need to implement it not like high level desing.\n    -> no need to ask the nfr etc. \n\n\n2. Understand the requirements: -> 10 -15mins\n\n\n\n3. Coding:  -> 40-50 minutes.\n\nUseful things: \n    -> in this cases usually we don't have complex implementation like dsa.\n    -> also in some cases its also fine if not very optimized , its should be running.\n       but yes exception are there, problem can be tough as well as\n       interviewer can ask for optimisation or optimize solution. \n\n    -> require oops language. \n    -> in this problem we might also need to deal with.\n        -> parsing, time, marshing, logging, string manipulation, input\n            output, random, math etc.\n    -> some time in this questions people also ask for api implementation\n        basically on the top of controller implement the apis. \n        -> sharechat. \n\n    -> you can even golang since you are writing code in golang in production\n        -> just make sure , fix one libraray for stl, since we can use the ide here\n            so its ok to using third party libraray for stl, they are not interested\n            to know whether you are able to writing code for sorting algo or red black tree. \n  ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
