      "text": "Now come the low level design",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Now come the low level design",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "_3zD1oHTzCsdqbCx2lk38",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1799.787202901839,
      "y": 1961.0658494622282,
      "width": 1136.5792236328125,
      "height": 1550,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aC",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 770830789,
      "version": 1420,
      "versionNonce": 231730059,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1766641397167,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "These interview mix of multiple things let go through each one by one. \n\nWhat would be interviewer want here ? \n-> according to me , he wants to have whether user would be alble to develop \n    quality service or not \n-> when we say quality -> readable, extendable, oops, follow design pattern, testcases,\n    api proper format, error handling, logging, retry , edge cases coverage, concurrency,\n    concurrency patter, optmised code etc.\n\n\nMistake People make and get rejected: \n-> Jumping Straight into Classes, But good design isn’t about boxes and arrows.\n    It’s about understanding the product before the code.\n    Fix this:\n    -> Start with the user journey.\n    -> What’s the flow? What are the use cases? What edge cases exist?\n    -> Once you map that, the classes will almost design themselves.\n-> Lack of OOP Principles in Action, Candidates know about SOLID, but forget to use it.\n-> LLD rounds aren’t scored just on completeness. They’re also scored on \n   how many red flags you raise even unintentionally. \n    -> What usually happens:\n            -> You walk out thinking, “I covered all the classes and flows.”\n            -> But behind the scenes, the interviewer notes:\n                ❌ Mixed business logic in the controller\n                ❌ No validation for null or edge scenarios\n                ❌ Inconsistent or unclear naming\n                ❌ Ignored error handling or retries\n            These red flags may seem small, but they hint at how you’d perform in a real-world engineering role.\n    -> Fix this: You don’t need to be perfect. You just need to show awareness.\n            -> Say things like:“We should validate user input here to prevent null pointer crashes. For error handling, \n               I’ll use a retry with exponential backoff for external API calls.”\n\n-> ✅ What Strong Candidates Do Differently?\n        They…\n        -> Start with product thinking → Who is using this? How?\n        -> Map flows first → then extract classes\n        -> Explain while designing → instead of silent sketching\n        -> Think in scenarios → happy path + edge + scale\n        -> Use patterns & principles → with purpose and show judgment\n    -> Most people get stuck because they have no structur, they improvise under pressure and lose coherence.\n        Strong performers use a repeatable structure, a thought process that brings clarity under pressure.\n    -> Your response to a LLD question is expected to have parity with the number of years of experience you have.\n\n\n\n\n1. Requirements: ( 60% game is here, you should be able to understand properly )\n    -> here requirement will be similar to machine coding refer from there. \n\n2. Now They can ask multiple things here: \n\n-> Api design\n   -> refer the hld template\n\n-> Db schema designing\n    -> refer hld section template\n\n-> Class designing. \n    -> So our focus would be this part only here. \n\n-> sudo code.\n    -> similar to machine coding",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "These interview mix of multiple things let go through each one by one. \n\nWhat would be interviewer want here ? \n-> according to me , he wants to have whether user would be alble to develop \n    quality service or not \n-> when we say quality -> readable, extendable, oops, follow design pattern, testcases,\n    api proper format, error handling, logging, retry , edge cases coverage, concurrency,\n    concurrency patter, optmised code etc.\n\n\nMistake People make and get rejected: \n-> Jumping Straight into Classes, But good design isn’t about boxes and arrows.\n    It’s about understanding the product before the code.\n    Fix this:\n    -> Start with the user journey.\n    -> What’s the flow? What are the use cases? What edge cases exist?\n    -> Once you map that, the classes will almost design themselves.\n-> Lack of OOP Principles in Action, Candidates know about SOLID, but forget to use it.\n-> LLD rounds aren’t scored just on completeness. They’re also scored on \n   how many red flags you raise even unintentionally. \n    -> What usually happens:\n            -> You walk out thinking, “I covered all the classes and flows.”\n            -> But behind the scenes, the interviewer notes:\n                ❌ Mixed business logic in the controller\n                ❌ No validation for null or edge scenarios\n                ❌ Inconsistent or unclear naming\n                ❌ Ignored error handling or retries\n            These red flags may seem small, but they hint at how you’d perform in a real-world engineering role.\n    -> Fix this: You don’t need to be perfect. You just need to show awareness.\n            -> Say things like:“We should validate user input here to prevent null pointer crashes. For error handling, \n               I’ll use a retry with exponential backoff for external API calls.”\n\n-> ✅ What Strong Candidates Do Differently?\n        They…\n        -> Start with product thinking → Who is using this? How?\n        -> Map flows first → then extract classes\n        -> Explain while designing → instead of silent sketching\n        -> Think in scenarios → happy path + edge + scale\n        -> Use patterns & principles → with purpose and show judgment\n    -> Most people get stuck because they have no structur, they improvise under pressure and lose coherence.\n        Strong performers use a repeatable structure, a thought process that brings clarity under pressure.\n    -> Your response to a LLD question is expected to have parity with the number of years of experience you have.\n\n\n\n\n1. Requirements: ( 60% game is here, you should be able to understand properly )\n    -> here requirement will be similar to machine coding refer from there. \n\n2. Now They can ask multiple things here: \n\n-> Api design\n   -> refer the hld template\n\n-> Db schema designing\n    -> refer hld section template\n\n-> Class designing. \n    -> So our focus would be this part only here. \n\n-> sudo code.\n    -> similar to machine coding",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "EgU_IIuVbesJ_7GIA6hKx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1789.2846077562413,
      "y": 3531.943886713218,
      "width": 1414.37890625,
      "height": 1975,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aD",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 504972299,
      "version": 3497,
      "versionNonce": 734492491,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1766641768272,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to make class diagram for lld interviews: \n\nThis is what we can follow: https://medium.com/@himanshusingour7/how-i-cracked-a-real-lld-interview-367b8a17ea88\n\n-> write now there are two things. if you already know db schema or don't know. \n\n-> most of the cases you don't know, very rare cases you know. Since interviewer either ask for db schema or class diagram \n    not both. \n\n\nStep 2: \n\n:> We have db schema:\n\n-> in this case just create the class entities.\n-> similar to machine coding. \n-> instead of id keep thing as object. \n\n-> in case of different type \n    -> create interface\n    -> and different implementations\n\n-> add some creation design pattern\n    -> like singleton\n    -> builder\n    -> factory.\n\n\n-> So just create the model , no need to have methods can have very simple methods.\n-> Next ?? Step 3\n\n\n:> We don't have db schema\n\n    -> this case is similar to machine coding round\n    -> you need to think in you mind as db schema\n    -> and write it as model or class ( instead of id its actually object ) \n    -> model is layer between service and dao , communication.\n    -> you need not to write the methods and all. \n\n\nStep 3: \n\n -> now tell interviewer i'll create the service layer that will have all functionality. \n -> create the multiple service layer, according to context like userservice, accountservice etc.\n -> make sure only write the things which matter, you need to show that you know things and know\n     how to solve problem instead of writing tons of files etc.\n\n -> create service class -> write methods in it , its input and output. \n -> sample: https://github.com/manvirag/tech_learning_tinkering/blob/main/lld/problems/category_b_machine_coding/parking_lot/design/image.png\n\n -> also tell that there would be dao layer as well for this ( optional to create )\n -> but to show that to separe concern. \n\n\n -> in case of lld we can draw controller as well , write the multiple service in it and methods.\n    -> here method are actually which is exposed to users.\n    -> most of the time service also have same function, but they have busniess logic\n        and controller are orchestrator validate, call service, status/error handling. \n\n\n-> later on you can tell \n        -> this is controller layer\n        -> this is service layer\n        -> this is dao loayer\n        -> service and dao will communicate using model ( input can we any way, return would be model)\n        -> just tell controller and service will communicate using dto/ or normal class or directly value sending. \n        -> you need not to create dto , just tell object as per request, basically these are the type to communicate\n            between controller - service layer. so that in future if any change on controller it wont' affer service\n            and similarly any change on service it wont' affect dao/repository.\n-> at each point make sure to handle the error / edge cases properly like you are writing the production code.\n-> later on can add the concurrency -> refer language specific concurrency for this.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to make class diagram for lld interviews: \n\nThis is what we can follow: https://medium.com/@himanshusingour7/how-i-cracked-a-real-lld-interview-367b8a17ea88\n\n-> write now there are two things. if you already know db schema or don't know. \n\n-> most of the cases you don't know, very rare cases you know. Since interviewer either ask for db schema or class diagram \n    not both. \n\n\nStep 2: \n\n:> We have db schema:\n\n-> in this case just create the class entities.\n-> similar to machine coding. \n-> instead of id keep thing as object. \n\n-> in case of different type \n    -> create interface\n    -> and different implementations\n\n-> add some creation design pattern\n    -> like singleton\n    -> builder\n    -> factory.\n\n\n-> So just create the model , no need to have methods can have very simple methods.\n-> Next ?? Step 3\n\n\n:> We don't have db schema\n\n    -> this case is similar to machine coding round\n    -> you need to think in you mind as db schema\n    -> and write it as model or class ( instead of id its actually object ) \n    -> model is layer between service and dao , communication.\n    -> you need not to write the methods and all. \n\n\nStep 3: \n\n -> now tell interviewer i'll create the service layer that will have all functionality. \n -> create the multiple service layer, according to context like userservice, accountservice etc.\n -> make sure only write the things which matter, you need to show that you know things and know\n     how to solve problem instead of writing tons of files etc.\n\n -> create service class -> write methods in it , its input and output. \n -> sample: https://github.com/manvirag/tech_learning_tinkering/blob/main/lld/problems/category_b_machine_coding/parking_lot/design/image.png\n\n -> also tell that there would be dao layer as well for this ( optional to create )\n -> but to show that to separe concern. \n\n\n -> in case of lld we can draw controller as well , write the multiple service in it and methods.\n    -> here method are actually which is exposed to users.\n    -> most of the time service also have same function, but they have busniess logic\n        and controller are orchestrator validate, call service, status/error handling. \n\n\n-> later on you can tell \n        -> this is controller layer\n        -> this is service layer\n        -> this is dao loayer\n        -> service and dao will communicate using model ( input can we any way, return would be model)\n        -> just tell controller and service will communicate using dto/ or normal class or directly value sending. \n        -> you need not to create dto , just tell object as per request, basically these are the type to communicate\n            between controller - service layer. so that in future if any change on controller it wont' affer service\n            and similarly any change on service it wont' affect dao/repository.\n-> at each point make sure to handle the error / edge cases properly like you are writing the production code.\n-> later on can add the concurrency -> refer language specific concurrency for this.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
